# Regulatory Flux Model — analysis code

Companion code for:

> Garbus, I. (2026). *Degradome Sequencing in Plants: From Cleavage Detection to
> Regulatory Flux.* [Journal, under review]

These scripts reproduce the Regulatory Flux Model (RFM) classification reported
in Section 5 of the manuscript: Tables 3 and 4, Figure 6, and Supplementary
Tables S1–S3.

---

## Contents

```
01_RFM_functions.R                 Core RFM functions (Box 1)
02_build_rfm_tables.R              CleaveLand4 parsing, RPKM, abundance filter
03_run_datasets.R                  Main entry point: runs the three datasets
04_export_supplementary_tables.R   Supplementary Tables S1-S3
05_test_RFM_functions.R            Unit checks

pipeline/
  prepare_degradome_inputs.sh      Adapter trimming and 20-nt truncation
                                   WITHOUT read collapsing (see Section 4)

data/
  dataset1_rfm_classified.csv      Yan et al. 2024,    9 pairs
  dataset2_rfm_classified.csv      German et al. 2008, 8 pairs
  dataset3_rfm_classified.csv      Shin et al. 2018, 108 pairs
```

The three CSV files in `data/` are the classified miRNA-target tables underlying
Tables 3 and 4 and Figure 6. Columns: `gene`, `miRNA`, `DegradomeCategory`,
`rpkm`, `CS`, `T`, `zone`. Copy them into `$BASE_DIR/results/` to reproduce the
classification without re-running the upstream sequencing pipeline.

The upstream steps (SRA retrieval, CleaveLand4, STAR, featureCounts) are fully
specified in Section 9 of the manuscript: accessions, software versions, and all
non-default parameters. Only `prepare_degradome_inputs.sh` is included here,
because the read-handling decision it implements is not a default and is
essential to reproducing the cleavage-site counts (Section 4 below).

---

## Requirements

R 4.3.3. Base R only (`stats`); no additional packages.

Upstream tools, for reference: CleaveLand4 4.5 with GSTAr 1.0, bowtie 1.3.1,
ViennaRNA 2.3.3, samtools 1.24, STAR 2.7.11b, subread (featureCounts) 2.1.1,
cutadapt 5.2, FASTX-Toolkit 0.0.14, seqtk 1.4, SRA Toolkit 3.4.1.

---

## Usage

```bash
export BASE_DIR=/path/to/working/directory   # must contain results/
export SCRIPT_DIR=/path/to/this/directory

Rscript 05_test_RFM_functions.R      # optional sanity check
Rscript 03_run_datasets.R            # RFM classification, Tables 3 and 4
Rscript 04_export_supplementary_tables.R
```

`03_run_datasets.R` sources `02_build_rfm_tables.R`, which sources
`01_RFM_functions.R`.

Place the three `data/*.csv` files in `$BASE_DIR/results/` to reproduce the
classification without re-running the upstream pipeline.

---

## RFM classification

Each miRNA–target pair is positioned by the residual of a descriptive linear
baseline fitted within each dataset:

- CS = 4 − DegradomeCategory (ordinal cleavage-confidence proxy)
- T = log₂(RPKM + 1)
- baseline: CS ~ T, fitted per dataset
- zone: residual > +1 SD → PTD; −1 SD to +1 SD → CR; < −1 SD → TD

Zones describe relative positions within a dataset. They are not validated
biological states and are not transferable across datasets without refitting.
Threshold sensitivity at ±0.5, ±1.0 and ±1.5 SD is reported in Table 4.

---

## Two analytical decisions that affect the results

**Reads are not collapsed before CleaveLand4.** CleaveLand4 infers cleavage
abundance from the number of FASTA entries aligned at each position; collapsing
to unique sequences counts hyper-abundant sites once, depressing the resulting
degradome categories. For Dataset 3 this raised the number of significant
cleavage sites from 47 to 148. Implemented in
`pipeline/prepare_degradome_inputs.sh`.

**Pairs with RPKM = 0 are excluded.** Zero mapped RNA-seq reads give T = 0, the
minimum of the abundance axis, so such pairs occupy the upper residual region by
construction rather than by cleavage evidence. This reduces Dataset 3 from 148 to
108 pairs and Dataset 2 from 10 to 8; Dataset 1 is unaffected. Implemented in
`02_build_rfm_tables.R` (filter `rpkm > 0`).

Both decisions are documented in Section 9 of the manuscript.

---

## Datasets

| Dataset | Degradome | RNA-seq | Reference |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | SRR28470273–75 | SRR28470277–78 | Yan *et al.* (2024) |
| 2 | SRR000222–23 | none | German *et al.* (2008) |
| 3 | SRR5713893 | SRR5713903 | Shin *et al.* (2018) |

Dataset 2 has no matched RNA-seq library; transcript abundance is taken from the
Dataset 1 floral RNA-seq, an explicit tissue mismatch. All its pairs are of
Category 4, so CS is constant, the baseline has no residual variance, and zone
assignment is undefined. It is retained only as a methodological
boundary-condition case (Supplementary Note S1).

